#!/bin/sh
#
# script/falsifications: what evidence each Kani harness carries that it can fail (milestone 194).
#
# PROVISIONAL beyond four names. DECISIONS §134 ratified `Falsification:`, the three states
# `replayable`/`attested`/`unfalsified`, this script's name, and the patch path
# `<package>/falsifications/<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch`. Every other spelling here (the
# flags, the column headings) is this lane's and an architect names things.
#
# Why this exists. notes/verification.md has said "falsify a property before believing it" since
# milestone 35, and milestone 191 measured what that produced: 141 harnesses and **not one of them
# recording what was done to falsify it**. The rule was rung four, honoured by whoever remembered,
# and the reverse pass found the predictable result. `capability::subset_is_reflexive` proves
# `a & !a == 0`, a tautology no plausible implementation error breaks, and twelve of the 26 `paging`
# harnesses restate six properties once per ISA.
#
# The forcing question is calef's and it is about a claim rather than about code: if this project is
# going to say **proven**, the evidence that the proofs are load-bearing cannot itself be a written
# claim that they are load-bearing. That is one level of indirection away from evidence, and it is
# the same shape as the nine misrecorded roadmap statuses and the fabricated block quote that
# survived twelve days of gates.
#
# **Inductive Validity Cores were checked first and are not available**, which is why this script
# exists in the shape it does. §134 named them as the cheaper family: an IVC is the minimal set of
# model elements a proof actually needed, so it gives coverage with no re-run at all. Neither Kani
# 0.67.0 nor the CBMC 6.8.0 it ships exposes anything of the kind, and the reason is structural
# rather than a missing feature: IVC is a technique of INDUCTIVE model checkers (JKind, Kind2, Sally)
# that prove by k-induction over a transition system, where the core falls out of the unsatisfiable
# induction step. Kani is a BOUNDED model checker that unrolls to a depth and hands one SAT formula
# to CaDiCaL, and a bounded proof has no induction step to take a core from. `cargo kani --coverage`
# is source-based REACHABILITY coverage (which regions symbolic execution touched), which catches
# some vacuity and no tautology at all; `cbmc --slice-formula` drops assignments unrelated to the
# property as a solver optimisation and reports nothing. See notes/verification.md.
#
# The three states, and the unknown one is first-class. This is `script/names`' lesson taken whole,
# and for the same reason: inventing evidence to fill a row puts a false claim in the one record
# whose entire job is saying what is actually known.
#
#   replayable <path>   a patch exists that a script applies to turn this harness red.
#                       --sweep applies it, runs that one harness, REQUIRES red, reverts.
#   attested <date>     a person broke the code and watched it fail; nothing can re-check it.
#                       Counted, and a worklist entry. An assertion is not evidence.
#   unfalsified         nobody has. This is the claim's honest denominator.
#
# **A kernel `#[test_case]` carries the same block and is swept the same way** (milestone 305).
# Milestone 202 recorded a falsification against
# `kernel::user::c_seam_tests::a_c_out_of_bounds_write_faults_and_changes_nothing_outside_its_grant`,
# a test that boots under QEMU rather than a Kani harness, and nothing could replay it: this script
# keyed on `#[kani::proof]`, and the only replay verb it had was `cargo kani --harness`. **Milestone
# 210 built the missing half** (`cargo xtask test --test <substring>` filters the kernel suite), and
# six kernel confinement claims in notes/confinement-claims.md had sat at "no mechanism" waiting for
# it. So the three states, the `Falsification:` block and §134's patch path are all unchanged; what
# is new is a second replay verb and a second thing to read the block above.
#
# **§134 is read as already covering this**, rather than amended, and the evidence is that milestone
# 202's patch filename obeys the section's spelling exactly with no special case:
# `<package>/falsifications/<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch` names
# `kernel/falsifications/user.c_seam_tests.a_c_...patch`. The only word that strains is `harness`,
# and it strains in prose rather than in the convention. If an architect wants the wording widened,
# that is
# a decisions edit and not this script's.
#
# **Kernel tests are opt-in where Kani harnesses are mandatory**, which is the one deliberate
# asymmetry. `--check` fails a `#[kani::proof]` that carries no block, because there are 145 of them
# and the whole point of the record is that every proof says which of the three it is. There are 312
# `#[test_case]`s and almost none of them is a confinement claim, so requiring a block on all of them
# would buy a wall of `unfalsified` and teach nobody anything. A kernel test is counted here when it
# carries a block, and the both-directions patch check is what keeps that honest: a patch under a
# package's `falsifications/` must be claimed by a harness **or** by a test, and an unclaimed one is
# still reported as rot.
#
# The block, which is all the lint checks:
#
#   /// Falsification: replayable `crates/capability/falsifications/verification.subset_is_transitive.patch`
#   /// Falsification: attested 2026-08-30. Flipped the mask; the subset assertion failed.
#   /// Falsification: unfalsified. Nobody has proposed a defect this would catch.
#
# It is the comment run immediately above `#[kani::proof]`, past any other attributes, in the shape
# milestone 115 established for `Name:`: at the thing, never in a registry, so two lanes touching
# two crates cannot collide and a computed report cannot drift from the tree.
#
#   script/falsifications                   the table, by package, and the ratio
#   script/falsifications --unfalsified     the worklist: harnesses with no evidence, biggest first
#   script/falsifications --check           form, plus "does each patch still apply"; non-zero on a
#                                           problem (script/lint runs this)
#   script/falsifications --count           two integers, harnesses and how many are falsified,
#                                           for the drift check in script/lint (milestone 236)
#   script/falsifications --sweep [package] apply every recorded patch, require red, revert
#   script/falsifications --affected-since <base>   sweep only what a diff can reach (the per-PR half)
#
# Both sweeps give each record **at most 15 minutes (a kernel test 30)** and report one that runs
# out as `ERROR ... no verdict`, then go on to the next record and exit non-zero at the end.
# `FALSIFICATIONS_RECORD_LIMIT=<seconds>` overrides both. Every verdict line carries its wall-clock
# time. Why those numbers is beside `LIMIT_KANI` below; the example that forced it is #1156's
# `nifefs` record, which held the weekly sweep for 1 h 44 min before the job was cancelled.
#
# **`--count` stays Kani-only and that is deliberate.** `script/lint`'s drift check (milestone 236)
# compares these two integers against `script/metrics`, which counts the same harnesses from source
# text at revisions nobody has checked out. Folding kernel tests into the numbers here would make the
# two derivations disagree for a reason neither is wrong about, which is the drift the check exists to
# find. The kernel-test ratio is printed in the table instead, as its own sentence.
#
# **The scope is every workspace package, and it was `crates/` until milestone 212.** That is the
# whole of that milestone and it was not an undercount so much as a mislabelled fraction: the ratio
# below is printed as a statement about the tree's proofs, and it described one directory. Milestone
# 193 (put `kernel/src` within reach of the prover) and milestone 197 (`user/` and `xtask` are out of
# reach of the prover) each put a package outside `crates/` into `script/verify`'s crate table, and
# both then grew harnesses and falsification records this script could not see. The package list now
# comes from `cargo metadata`, which is where `script/lint`'s verify-table check already gets the
# same fact, because a hand-maintained list is the same defect one iteration later: `script/verify`
# has recorded that failure twice (`multicast_dns_protocol`, then `jh7110_entropy`), and it is the invisible
# direction, since a suite whose scope is too small goes green FASTER.
#
# **The gate is presence, never `replayable`.** Nothing here fails a build because a harness has no
# falsification yet, and it must stay that way, for exactly the reason `script/names --check` gates on
# presence rather than on `ratified`: a lint demanding the queue be drained would block every
# unrelated merge behind work nobody can hurry. The worklist is how the queue gets worked; the gate
# only insists a harness say which of the three it is.
#
# **The sweep's posture is `script/mutation`'s, deliberately copied**: a report against a baseline,
# not a per-commit gate, because a full run costs about one `script/verify` and a regression is a
# worklist entry rather than a defect in whatever landed that day. `--affected-since` is the half
# `script/mutation` has no equivalent for, and it is cheap because a falsification runs ONE harness.
#
# BUGS.
#
# - **Nothing forces the ratio upward.** Every harness may sit at `unfalsified` for ever while
#   `--check` stays green. That is the honest cost of making the convention shippable against 141
#   existing harnesses at all, and it is why the number that matters is the ratio rather than the
#   gate.
# - **A recorded falsification proves the harness catches THAT defect, not the class.** It is a
#   floor, and a low one. A patch that flips a comparison proves the assertion is wired to the
#   comparison; it says nothing about the property being the right property.
# - **A patch rots against refactors**, and §134 argues that is correct rather than harmless: a patch
#   that no longer applies means the covered code moved, which is exactly when a falsification should
#   be redone rather than trusted. It is still churn, and a heavily refactored crate re-falsifies
#   often. `--sweep` reports a patch that will not apply as a failure, not as a skip, and since
#   2026-09-25 `--check` does too, so the pull request that moves the code is the one that fails.
#   Before that, only the weekly sweep looked, and its job reports success whatever it finds
#   (`continue-on-error`, by design): three x86 port records sat stale from the merge of
#   milestone 315 (a port revoke that reaches every core) until a rename lane tripped over them.
#   Applying is not red; a re-cut patch still wants a replay.
# - **The limit is a wall clock on a shared machine, so load can turn a slow record into a false
#   `no verdict`.** 15 minutes is 1.8 times the slowest record measured (497 s, hosted runner,
#   build included), which is headroom rather than a margin anyone proved. A record that times out
#   on a busy laptop and not in CI is load; one that times out in CI wants a faster falsification or
#   a bound on its harness, which is what `nifefs::a_short_image_is_refused_not_indexed` got
#   (`#[kani::unwind(9)]`, measured to change nothing on the correct tree). The limit also does not
#   distinguish a record that would have finished at minute 16 from one that never would; both are
#   reported the same way, because neither is evidence.
# - **`--sweep` refuses to run on a dirty working tree**, because it applies and reverts patches with
#   `git apply` and a failure mid-run would otherwise leave a deliberate defect in somebody's source.
#   That is a real limit: you cannot sweep while you work.
# - **A kernel-test falsification costs a boot, and what it really costs is whatever the patch makes
#   cargo rebuild.** Measured on patagonia, 2026-09-16: the whole 48-record sweep is **2 min 55 s**
#   warm, against about **22 min** on a tree where each patch forced a rebuild. Milestone 210
#   measured QEMU start to `running N tests` at 0.50 s and a warm filtered run at 8.6 s, and a
#   riscv64 record here came in at 9.9 s, so the boot is not the cost and never was. A patch that
#   touches a crate the kernel and the user programs both depend on pays for the archive, the
#   standard-library exerciser and five disk images on every apply and every revert. That is why
#   this stayed a report rather than becoming a gate.
# - **`--affected-since` covers only the Kani half.** It is the per-PR half and the reason it is
#   affordable is that a falsification runs one harness; adding a per-record kernel boot would put
#   eight minutes on every pull request that touches `kernel/`. So a kernel-test falsification is
#   re-checked by a full `--sweep` and by nothing else, which means it can rot between sweeps exactly
#   the way a Kani record cannot.
# - **A kernel record is swept on ONE architecture, the one its patch names.** `Architecture:` in the
#   patch head (default `aarch64`) picks the leg, because a defect is not always expressible on all
#   three: `crates/paging`'s `U` bit marks a page user-accessible on RISC-V, and a kernel page marked
#   `U` faults the kernel itself with `sstatus.SUM` clear, so "the kernel maps its own memory
#   user-readable" cannot be booted there at all. DECISIONS §19 makes parity a gate for the
#   *capability*; this is a gap in the *evidence*, and notes/confinement-claims.md names which rows
#   it affects.
# - **A kernel record's red is checked for shape, not for the assertion it names.** The sweep
#   requires the kernel to have booted and selected exactly one test (`running 1 of N`) before it
#   will call a non-zero exit red, which is what keeps a patch that does not compile, or a boot that
#   dies, from reading as a successful falsification. It cannot check that the failure came through
#   the assertion the patch's prose predicts; the transcript's panic line is printed so a reader can.
#   Milestone 202's watchdog timeout at 234 seconds is the failure this leaves on the table.
# - **The affected-since computation is crate-granular, not harness-granular.** A change anywhere in
#   a harness crate's dependency closure re-falsifies every replayable harness in that crate, because
#   nothing in this tree knows which lines a given harness actually covers (that is what an IVC would
#   have bought). It fails toward doing too much.
# - **The sweep replays a harness only on a host whose architecture compiles it** (milestone 304),
#   and nothing here says which host a record needs. `kernel/src/arch/mod.rs` selects its subtree
#   with `#[cfg(target_arch = ...)]` and Kani compiles for the host, so
#   `kernel/falsifications/arch.aarch64.iommu.*.patch` can only be replayed on an aarch64 box and an
#   `arch.x86_64.*` one only on an x86_64 box. That has been true since milestone 255 wrote the
#   first of them and went unnoticed because every machine in this project was aarch64; milestone
#   304 added an x86_64 CI runner and made it a fact with two sides. **The honest consequence is
#   that an architecture-specific harness records `attested` rather than `replayable`** (both of
#   milestone 304's do, and say why at the harness), because a `replayable` record here would turn
#   `--sweep kernel` red on every host but one. Teaching the sweep to skip a patch it cannot compile
#   is the obvious fix and is deliberately NOT taken: a skip is the invisible direction this file
#   argues against everywhere else, and it would need a per-record statement of the host it wants,
#   which is a format change and so calef's.
# - **`kernel/src/arch/` is barely covered here**, and the line above this one used to say it carried
#   no harnesses at all, which stopped being true when milestone 255 proved
#   `arch/aarch64/iommu.rs` in place. It is now six harnesses across three architectures' files
#   since milestone 432 (the RISC-V IOMMU driver has no counterpart to the SMMU's proofs) added
#   riscv64's `iommu.rs`, replayable on an aarch64 host like aarch64's own. The timer re-arm
#   drift, the reason this entry exists, is still on the far side of the `asm!` boundary and
#   outside this record entirely. (This entry used to pair the timer drift with
#   "the VisionFive 2's undelivered-wake defect." That reading is retracted, notes/visionfive2.md's
#   fifth bench stop, 2026-08-15: it was a completed tour's terminal state, not a stranded receiver,
#   and never happened. Found still repeating it here 2026-09-23.)
# - **A file's module path is derived from the Cargo target it belongs to, and one shape defeats
#   that.** `components/src` holds 49 `[[bin]]` roots and two single-consumer `#[path]` modules that rule 7
#   permits (`net_transport.rs`, `socket_test_client.rs`). A root contributes no module segment and a
#   `#[path]` module contributes whatever the including file calls it, which need not be its
#   filename. Neither carries a harness today; one that arrived in a `#[path]` module would get a
#   patch path naming the file rather than the module, and `--check` would report it as a path that
#   does not match rather than accept it silently.
# - **Two files carrying `#[kani::proof]` are in no workspace package and are skipped**: the
#   `helpers/kani-lint-shim` source `script/lint` compiles by hand, and `vendor/redoxfs`, kept out of
#   the workspace on purpose. Neither is provable, so neither belongs in a ratio about proofs.
#   `script/lint`'s verify-table check is what keeps that honest, by failing on any harness file
#   belonging to no package with exactly those two argued as exemptions; this script does not keep a
#   second copy of the list.
#
# Name: ratified 2026-08-30 (calef, DECISIONS §134). The `script/` family splits in a way nobody had
# written down until that section: what DOES something is a verb (`verify`, `test`, `fuzz`, `bench`)
# and what REPORTS is a noun (`names`, `citations`, `decisions`, `roadmap`, `coverage`, `mutation`).
# §134 decided a report with a baseline rather than a gate, so this belongs in the noun half. Refused
# `falsify`, whose verb form would promise the gate §134 deliberately did not build.

set -e

ROOT="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)"
cd "$ROOT"

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why PATH is
# the mechanism). A kernel-test record boots through `cargo xtask test`, and until 2026-09-25 this
# script was missing from `script/lint`'s check for the line: it builds that command as a Python
# list, which the check's pattern does not see. A no-op on macOS and without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

exec python3 - "$ROOT" "$@" <<'PY'
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# **Line-buffered, because a sweep that is killed must still say what it replayed.** Python blocks
# stdout when it is not a terminal, so every `==> package::harness` line of a CI run sat in a buffer
# and died with the process. On 2026-09-23 the per-pull-request replay on #1156 ran the full 45
# minutes of `verify.yml`'s `timeout-minutes` and was cancelled mid-CBMC having printed **nothing**:
# 44 new records, and no way to tell which of them had been proved red before the axe fell. A
# cancelled run is the case where the transcript matters most, and it was the one case that had
# none.
sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)

root = sys.argv[1]
args = sys.argv[2:]
mode = args[0] if args else ""

STATES = ("replayable", "attested", "unfalsified")

# A `#[kani::proof]` attribute, with or without the `_for_contract` suffix and with or without
# arguments. Matched on the stripped line so indentation inside `mod verification` does not matter.
PROOF = re.compile(r"^#\[kani::proof")
ATTR = re.compile(r"^#\[")
FN = re.compile(r"^(?:pub\s+)?(?:unsafe\s+)?fn\s+(\w+)")
COMMENT = re.compile(r"^//")
RECORD = re.compile(r"^//[/!]?\s*Falsification:\s*(.*)$")

# The first line of a patch that falsifies something `cargo kani` cannot run: `Falsifies
# `kernel::user::c_seam_tests::a_c_out_of_bounds_write_faults...``. See `unclaimed()`.
FALSIFIES = re.compile(r"^Falsifies\s+`?([A-Za-z_][\w:]*)`?", re.M)

# A kernel `#[test_case]`, matched on the stripped line exactly as `PROOF` is (milestone 305).
TEST_CASE = re.compile(r"^#\[test_case\]")

# `Architecture: riscv64` in a kernel patch's prose head: which leg the sweep boots. Default
# `aarch64`. See this script's BUGS on why a kernel record is swept on one architecture.
ARCH_DECL = re.compile(r"^Architecture:\s*(\w+)\s*$", re.M)
ARCHES = ("aarch64", "riscv64", "x86_64")

MOD_OPEN = re.compile(r"^(?:pub(?:\([^)]*\))?\s+)?mod\s+(\w+)\s*\{")


_META = {}


def metadata(no_deps):
    """`cargo metadata`, cached, because three code paths here want it and it is not cheap.

    This is the whole of milestone 212. The walk used to be `os.walk("crates")`, so the ratio this
    script prints described one directory and was printed as a fraction of the tree. `kernel` and
    `user` are workspace packages at the repository root, both are rows in `script/verify`'s crate
    table since milestones 193 (put `kernel/src` within reach of the prover) and 197 (`user/` and
    `xtask` are out of reach of the prover), and both carry harnesses the old walk could not see.

    Derived rather than listed, for the reason `script/verify`'s own table comment gives twice over:
    `multicast_dns_protocol` and then `jh7110_entropy` each carried harnesses nothing ran, and that failure is the
    invisible one, because the suite goes green FASTER when a scope is too small.
    """
    key = bool(no_deps)
    if key not in _META:
        cmd = ["cargo", "metadata", "--format-version", "1", "--locked"]
        if no_deps:
            cmd.append("--no-deps")
        _META[key] = json.loads(subprocess.run(
            cmd, cwd=root, capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout)
    return _META[key]


class Package:
    """A workspace package: its name, the directory it lives in, and its Cargo targets.

    The targets are what make the module path right for a package of binaries. `user` is 68
    `[[bin]]` targets sharing one `src/` directory, so `components/src/printenv.rs` is a CRATE ROOT and
    contributes no module segment, where `crates/paging/src/sv39.rs` sits below a `lib` root and
    contributes `sv39`. The old derivation read the path (`parts[2] == "src"`) and would have
    called the first one `printenv::proofs::...`, which is not a path Kani would answer to.
    """

    def __init__(self, name, reldir, targets, has_global_asm):
        self.name = name
        self.reldir = reldir            # "crates/paging", "kernel", "user"
        self.prefix = reldir + "/"
        self.targets = targets          # [(target_name, kind, rel_src_path), ...]
        self.has_global_asm = has_global_asm

    @property
    def bins(self):
        return [t for t in self.targets if t[1] == "bin"]

    def target_of(self, rel):
        """The target whose sources `rel` belongs to, and whether `rel` is that target's root."""
        if rel.endswith(".rs"):
            for t in self.targets:
                if t[2] == rel:
                    return t, True
        best = None
        for t in self.targets:
            d = os.path.dirname(t[2]) + "/"
            if rel.startswith(d) and (best is None or len(d) > len(os.path.dirname(best[2]) + "/")):
                best = t
        return best, False

    def file_mods(self, rel):
        """The module segments a FILE contributes, from the crate root down.

        `crates/paging/src/sv39.rs` under a `lib.rs` root is `["sv39"]`; `components/src/printenv.rs`,
        which IS a root, is `[]`. The rest of a harness's path comes from the inline `mod X {`
        blocks, which is where `verification` and `proofs` come from.
        """
        target, is_root = self.target_of(rel)
        if target is None or is_root:
            return []
        base = os.path.dirname(target[2]) + "/"
        tail = rel[len(base):].split("/")
        tail[-1] = tail[-1][: -len(".rs")]
        if tail[-1] in ("lib", "mod", "main"):
            tail = tail[:-1]
        return tail


def packages():
    """Every workspace package, longest-prefix-first so ownership of a path is unambiguous."""
    meta = metadata(no_deps=True)
    ws = meta["workspace_root"]
    out = []
    for p in meta["packages"]:
        rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.dirname(p["manifest_path"]), ws)
        if rel == "." or rel.startswith(".."):
            continue
        targets = []
        for t in p["targets"]:
            src = os.path.relpath(t["src_path"], ws)
            if src.startswith(".."):
                continue
            targets.append((t["name"], t["kind"][0] if t["kind"] else "?", src))
        # One `cargo kani` flag depends on this and it is derived rather than named, because a
        # package list in this file is the defect milestone 212 exists to remove. Kani refuses a
        # crate containing `global_asm!` outright unless told to ignore it, and `script/verify`
        # carries the same exception for the same reason; see its `kernel)` case and
        # notes/kernel-proofs.md for what the resulting stubs do and do not cover.
        asm = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-l", "global_asm!", "--", rel + "/*.rs"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True).returncode == 0
        out.append(Package(p["name"], rel, targets, asm))
    out.sort(key=lambda p: -len(p.prefix))
    return out


def module_path_by_line(file_mods, lines):
    """For each line, the module path from the crate root, as a list of segments.

    Two sources, and both are needed. The **file** contributes what `Package.file_mods` computed
    from the Cargo target it belongs to. The **inline `mod X {` blocks** contribute the rest, which
    is where `verification` comes from, since almost every harness in this tree lives in a
    `#[cfg(kani)] mod verification`.

    Brace counting rather than a Rust parser, which is the right size of tool here and is honest
    about its limit: a brace inside a string or a comment would miscount. Nothing in this tree does
    that at module scope, and a miscount surfaces as a patch path that does not match, which
    `--check` reports rather than silently accepting.
    """
    out = []
    stack = []       # [(depth_at_open, name), ...]
    depth = 0
    for line in lines:
        out.append(file_mods + [n for _, n in stack])
        hit = MOD_OPEN.match(line.strip())
        opened = hit.group(1) if hit else None
        for ch in line:
            if ch == "{":
                depth += 1
                if opened is not None:
                    stack.append((depth, opened))
                    opened = None
            elif ch == "}":
                while stack and stack[-1][0] == depth:
                    stack.pop()
                depth -= 1
    return out


def harness_files():
    """Every tracked `.rs` file carrying a `#[kani::proof]`, from `git grep` rather than a walk.

    Two files in this tree hold the attribute and belong to no workspace package: the
    `helpers/kani-lint-shim` source `script/lint` compiles by hand for its harness-clippy pass, and
    `vendor/redoxfs`, upstream code kept out of the workspace on purpose. Neither is provable, so
    neither belongs in a ratio about proofs. They are skipped here rather than listed, because
    `script/lint` already gates that direction: its verify-table check reports any file carrying
    `#[kani::proof]` that belongs to no workspace package, with those two named as the argued
    exemptions. A second copy of that list here would be a second thing to keep true.
    """
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-lE", r"#\[kani::proof", "--", "*.rs"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True)
    return sorted(out.stdout.split())


def test_case_files():
    """Every tracked `.rs` file carrying a `#[test_case]`, from `git grep` (milestone 305).

    Narrowed to files that ALSO mention `Falsification:` before anything is parsed, because a kernel
    test is opt-in: 312 `#[test_case]`s exist and a handful carry a record, so scanning the rest
    would be work with nothing at the end of it.
    """
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-lE", r"#\[test_case\]", "--", "*.rs"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True)
    files = []
    for rel in sorted(out.stdout.split()):
        with open(os.path.join(root, rel), errors="replace") as f:
            if "Falsification:" in f.read():
                files.append(rel)
    return files


def harnesses(pkgs=None):
    """Every Kani harness in a workspace package, with the falsification record it carries.

    Derived from the tree rather than from a list, for the reason `script/verify`'s own scope
    computation gives: a harness that arrives tomorrow is covered without editing anything, and a
    list that is one entry short fails silently and invisibly.
    """
    pkgs = pkgs if pkgs is not None else packages()
    out = []
    for rel in harness_files():
        pkg = next((p for p in pkgs if rel.startswith(p.prefix)), None)
        if pkg is None:
            continue
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        lines = open(path, errors="replace").read().split("\n")
        mods = module_path_by_line(pkg.file_mods(rel), lines)
        target, _ = pkg.target_of(rel)
        for i, line in enumerate(lines):
            if not PROOF.match(line.strip()):
                continue
            # Downward past any further attributes (`#[kani::unwind(8)]`, `#[kani::solver(..)]`)
            # to the function this proves. An attribute rustfmt wrapped over several lines (a
            # long `#[kani::stub(..)]`) is skipped to its closing `)]`; before 2026-09-25 one read
            # as a harness named "?" and dropped out of the count.
            name = None
            open_attr = False
            for j in range(i + 1, len(lines)):
                s = lines[j].strip()
                if open_attr:
                    open_attr = not s.endswith(")]")
                    continue
                if ATTR.match(s) and not s.endswith("]"):
                    open_attr = True
                    continue
                if not s or ATTR.match(s) or COMMENT.match(s):
                    continue
                hit = FN.match(s)
                name = hit.group(1) if hit else None
                break
            if name is None:
                out.append(Harness(pkg, target, "?", rel, i + 1, None, None, mods[i]))
                continue
            # Upward past the other attributes to the comment run above them, which is where
            # the block lives. Blank lines end it: a comment separated from the harness by a
            # gap belongs to whatever is above the gap.
            record, record_line = None, None
            k = i - 1
            while k >= 0 and ATTR.match(lines[k].strip()):
                k -= 1
            while k >= 0 and COMMENT.match(lines[k].strip()):
                hit = RECORD.match(lines[k].strip())
                if hit:
                    record, record_line = hit.group(1).strip(), k + 1
                k -= 1
            out.append(Harness(pkg, target, name, rel, i + 1, record, record_line, mods[i]))
    out.sort(key=lambda h: (h.package, h.name))
    return out


def kernel_tests(pkgs=None):
    """Every `#[test_case]` that CARRIES a `Falsification:` block (milestone 305).

    The same walk `harnesses()` does, with two differences and both are the asymmetry the header
    argues for. The attribute is `#[test_case]`, and a function without a record is skipped rather
    than reported, because the record is opt-in here: `--check` demands a block from all 145 Kani
    harnesses and would buy nothing but noise demanding one from 312 kernel tests.
    """
    pkgs = pkgs if pkgs is not None else packages()
    out = []
    for rel in test_case_files():
        pkg = next((p for p in pkgs if rel.startswith(p.prefix)), None)
        if pkg is None:
            continue
        lines = open(os.path.join(root, rel), errors="replace").read().split("\n")
        mods = module_path_by_line(pkg.file_mods(rel), lines)
        target, _ = pkg.target_of(rel)
        for i, line in enumerate(lines):
            if not TEST_CASE.match(line.strip()):
                continue
            # Downward past any further attributes (`#[cfg(target_arch = "aarch64")]`) to the
            # function, and upward past them to the comment run, exactly as `harnesses()` does.
            name = None
            for j in range(i + 1, len(lines)):
                s = lines[j].strip()
                if not s or ATTR.match(s) or COMMENT.match(s):
                    continue
                hit = FN.match(s)
                name = hit.group(1) if hit else None
                break
            if name is None:
                continue
            record, record_line = None, None
            k = i - 1
            while k >= 0 and ATTR.match(lines[k].strip()):
                k -= 1
            while k >= 0 and COMMENT.match(lines[k].strip()):
                hit = RECORD.match(lines[k].strip())
                if hit:
                    record, record_line = hit.group(1).strip(), k + 1
                k -= 1
            if record is None:
                continue
            out.append(KernelTest(pkg, target, name, rel, i + 1, record, record_line, mods[i]))
    out.sort(key=lambda h: (h.package, h.name))
    return out


class Harness:
    # What replays this record, which is the only thing `KernelTest` changes. Named rather than
    # tested with `isinstance`, because it is printed in the table and in the sweep's own output.
    kind = "kani"

    def __init__(self, pkg, target, name, rel, line, record, record_line, mods):
        self.pkg = pkg
        self.package = pkg.name
        self.target = target
        self.mods = mods
        self.name = name
        self.rel = rel
        self.line = line
        self.record = record
        self.record_line = record_line

    @property
    def state(self):
        """The first word of the record, with any trailing punctuation off it.

        The strip is load-bearing rather than tidy, and milestone 202 found it by writing the
        block this script's own header offers as an example: `unfalsified. Nobody has proposed a
        defect this would catch.` `attested` already tolerated the prose after its date, so only
        the bare states rejected the documented form, and the rejection read as a state nobody
        defined rather than as a punctuation rule. A convention whose reference example fails its
        own gate teaches the wrong thing to whoever hits it first.
        """
        if self.record is None:
            return None
        words = self.record.split()
        return words[0].rstrip(".,;:") if words else None

    @property
    def patch(self):
        """The repo-relative patch path a `replayable` record names, or None."""
        if self.state != "replayable":
            return None
        parts = self.record.split()
        # Backticks are stripped rather than required or forbidden. The path must be backticked in
        # a `///` doc comment or `clippy::doc_markdown` rejects it as an unmarked item path, and
        # `script/lint`'s kani-shim pass compiles exactly these `#[cfg(kani)]` modules, so it is the
        # gate that finds it. A `//` comment has no such rule. Accepting both keeps one form in the
        # record rather than one form per comment style.
        return parts[1].strip("`") if len(parts) > 1 else None

    @property
    def qualified(self):
        """The harness's fully qualified name inside its crate, `::`-joined.

        This is the string `cargo kani --harness ... --exact` wants, which is why the patch path is
        keyed on it: the path and the sweep's own filter are one fact with the separators changed.
        """
        return "::".join(self.mods + [self.name])

    @property
    def conventional_patch(self):
        # DECISIONS §134 as amended 2026-08-31 and clarified 2026-09-01:
        # `<package>/falsifications/<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch`, with the module path
        # ALWAYS included and no branch. The first spelling keyed on the function name alone, which
        # `paging` broke on contact: six properties stated once per ISA share six function names
        # across `aarch64.rs`, `sv39.rs` and `x86_64.rs`, so three harnesses would collide on one
        # file. Qualifying only when a name repeats was refused for being a branch on an unstable
        # property, since a harness added elsewhere would retroactively invalidate an existing path.
        #
        # The directory is the PACKAGE's, not `crates/<crate>`: `kernel` and `user` are packages at
        # the repository root and both already hold `falsifications/`, which §134's clarification
        # recognises.
        return "%s/falsifications/%s.patch" % (
            self.pkg.reldir, ".".join(self.mods + [self.name]))

    def kani_argv(self):
        """What `cargo kani` needs to run this ONE harness and nothing else.

        Two package-shaped facts, both derived. A package of many binaries needs `--bin`, because
        `cargo kani -p components` compiles all 49 and fails on the first `#![no_std]` root that does not
        mention Kani; `script/verify` solves the same problem by deriving a `--bin` list, and a
        sweep wants exactly one. A package carrying `global_asm!` needs it ignored, or Kani refuses
        to compile the crate at all.
        """
        argv = ["cargo", "kani", "-p", self.package]
        if self.pkg.has_global_asm:
            argv += ["-Z", "unstable-options", "--ignore-global-asm", "-Z", "stubbing"]
        if self.target is not None and self.target[1] == "bin" and len(self.pkg.bins) > 1:
            argv += ["--bin", self.target[0]]
        return argv + ["--harness", self.qualified, "--exact", "--output-format=terse"]


class KernelTest(Harness):
    """A kernel `#[test_case]` carrying a `Falsification:` block (milestone 305).

    Everything about the record is a `Harness`'s: the three states, the `<module.path>.<fn>.patch`
    spelling, the prose requirement. What differs is how it is replayed, which is milestone 210's
    filter rather than `cargo kani`, and that it boots a machine to do it.
    """

    kind = "kernel test"

    @property
    def architecture(self):
        """Which leg the sweep boots, from `Architecture:` in the patch's prose head.

        Defaulted rather than required, because `aarch64` is what every one of these was written
        against and a record that says nothing is saying that. A spelling that is not one of the
        three is a `--check` problem rather than a silent fallback: the sweep would otherwise run a
        leg nobody asked for and call the result evidence.
        """
        path = self.patch
        if not path or not os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, path)):
            return "aarch64"
        body = open(os.path.join(root, path), errors="replace").read()
        hit = ARCH_DECL.search(body.split("diff --git")[0])
        return hit.group(1) if hit else "aarch64"

    def replay_argv(self):
        """What runs this ONE test and nothing else: milestone 210's filter, on one architecture.

        The filter is the bare function name rather than the qualified path. Both work (the runner
        matches a substring of the full test path) and the short one is what a reader would type,
        which matters because this string is also the command a person re-runs by hand after the
        sweep reports a survivor.
        """
        return ["cargo", "xtask", "test", "--arch", self.architecture, "--test", self.name]


def patch_files(pkgs):
    """Every patch under a package's `falsifications/`, repo-relative."""
    out = []
    for pkg in sorted(pkgs, key=lambda p: p.reldir):
        d = os.path.join(root, pkg.reldir, "falsifications")
        if not os.path.isdir(d):
            continue
        for f in sorted(os.listdir(d)):
            if f.endswith(".patch"):
                out.append("%s/falsifications/%s" % (pkg.reldir, f))
    return out


def unclaimed(path, pkgs):
    """Classify a patch no harness claims: a known gap, or rot.

    §134 checks both directions, and the reason a patch with no harness is normally a PROBLEM is
    rot: the harness was renamed or deleted and the patch is now a file nobody runs.

    **One patch in this tree is neither**, and pretending otherwise would put a hardcoded exemption
    in the script whose whole milestone was removing a hardcoded scope. Milestone 202 (every
    confinement test is a ritual until somebody breaks the confinement) recorded a falsification of
    `kernel::user::c_seam_tests::a_c_out_of_bounds_write_faults_and_changes_nothing_outside_its_grant`,
    which is a `#[test_case]` that boots under QEMU rather than a Kani harness. It is a real record
    of a real falsification and nothing can sweep it until milestone 210 (no kernel test can be run
    by name) lands.

    So the distinction is drawn mechanically rather than by name. A patch may open with
    `Falsifies `<fully::qualified::name>``, and it is a **known gap** when that name agrees with the
    filename and resolves to a function that exists in the package and carries no `#[kani::proof]`.
    Everything else is rot. That keeps the rot check sharp in the direction that matters: a renamed
    or deleted harness fails, because the name no longer resolves to a function at all.

    Returns (kind, detail), where kind is "gap" or "rot".
    """
    body = open(os.path.join(root, path), errors="replace").read()
    head = body.split("diff --git")[0]
    hit = FALSIFIES.search(head)
    stem = os.path.basename(path)[: -len(".patch")]
    pkgdir = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(path))
    pkg = next((p for p in pkgs if p.reldir == pkgdir), None)
    if hit is None or pkg is None:
        return "rot", "no harness claims it and it declares no `Falsifies` target"
    target = hit.group(1)
    segments = target.split("::")
    if segments and segments[0] == pkg.name:
        segments = segments[1:]
    if ".".join(segments) != stem:
        return "rot", ("it declares `Falsifies %s`, which spells the filename `%s.patch`, not `%s`"
                       % (target, ".".join(segments), os.path.basename(path)))
    fn = segments[-1] if segments else ""
    found = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-lE",
         r"fn[[:space:]]+%s[[:space:]]*[(<]" % re.escape(fn), "--", pkg.reldir],
        capture_output=True, text=True)
    if found.returncode != 0:
        return "rot", ("it falsifies `%s`, and no function of that name exists in `%s` any more"
                       % (target, pkg.reldir))
    return "gap", "%s (%s), which no Kani harness declares" % (target, found.stdout.split()[0])


def check(hs, pkgs, kts=()):
    """Form, never truth. Exactly the line `script/names --check` draws, and for the same reason.

    Both directions, which §134 asked for by name: every `replayable` path resolves AND every patch
    under a package's `falsifications/` has a harness that claims it. A one-directional check lets a
    patch rot into a file nobody runs, which is the failure this record exists to end wearing
    different clothes.

    `kts` are the kernel tests carrying a record (milestone 305). They go through the same checks and
    into the same `claimed` set, which is what retires the "falsifies something that is not a Kani
    harness; nothing sweeps it" line: the patch now has a claimant and something does sweep it.
    """
    problems = []
    claimed = {}
    # One `git apply --check` per replayable patch, run in parallel up front: serially they doubled
    # this check's wall time (3.1 s to 5.6 s on patagonia, 2026-09-25, 144 patches).
    import concurrent.futures
    wanted = sorted({x.patch for x in list(hs) + list(kts)
                     if x.state == "replayable" and x.patch
                     and os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, x.patch))})
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:
        apply_checks = dict(zip(wanted, pool.map(
            lambda q: subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "apply", "--check", os.path.join(root, q)],
                                     capture_output=True, text=True), wanted)))
    for t in kts:
        if t.state == "replayable" and t.patch and t.architecture not in ARCHES:
            problems.append("%s:%d: `%s` declares `Architecture: %s`; the three are %s"
                            % (t.rel, t.line, t.patch, t.architecture, ", ".join(ARCHES)))
    hs = list(hs) + list(kts)
    # A duplicate qualified name is impossible in Rust, so this is a guard on the PARSER rather than
    # on the tree: if brace counting ever miscomputes a module path, two harnesses collapse onto one
    # patch path and the sweep would prove the wrong one. Cheap to assert, invisible if it drifts.
    seen = {}
    for h in hs:
        seen[h.conventional_patch] = seen.get(h.conventional_patch, 0) + 1
    for h in hs:
        where = "%s:%d" % (h.rel, h.line)
        if h.name == "?":
            problems.append("%s: a `#[kani::proof]` whose function this script could not find"
                            % where)
            continue
        if h.record is None:
            problems.append("%s: harness `%s` carries no `Falsification:` block. One of: "
                            "`replayable <path>`, `attested <YYYY-MM-DD>`, `unfalsified`."
                            % (where, h.name))
            continue
        state = h.state
        if state not in STATES:
            problems.append("%s: harness `%s` has state `%s`; the three are %s"
                            % (where, h.name, state, ", ".join("`%s`" % s for s in STATES)))
            continue
        if state == "replayable":
            if seen[h.conventional_patch] > 1:
                problems.append(
                    "%s: %d harnesses compute the patch path `%s`, which cannot happen in Rust and "
                    "so means this script's module-path tracking is wrong. Do not record a "
                    "falsification against it until that is fixed; the sweep would prove the wrong "
                    "harness." % (where, seen[h.conventional_patch], h.conventional_patch))
                continue
            path = h.patch
            if not path:
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` is `replayable` and names no patch" % (where, h.name))
            elif path != h.conventional_patch:
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` names `%s`; §134 spells it `%s`"
                                % (where, h.name, path, h.conventional_patch))
            elif not os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, path)):
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` names `%s`, which does not exist"
                                % (where, h.name, path))
            else:
                claimed[path] = h.name
                body = open(os.path.join(root, path), errors="replace").read()
                head = body.split("diff --git")[0].strip()
                if "diff --git" not in body:
                    problems.append("%s: `%s` holds no `diff --git` hunk for `git apply`" % (where, path))
                elif not head:
                    problems.append(
                        "%s: `%s` opens with no prose. A falsification that makes a harness fail "
                        "for the wrong reason proves nothing, so the patch must say which property "
                        "it expects to break, above the first `diff --git` line (git apply ignores it)."
                        % (where, path))
                else:
                    # Does it still apply? The one question about a record's truth this check asks,
                    # because it costs a `git apply --check` (milliseconds) rather than a boot, and
                    # because the pull request that moved the covered code is the one place the
                    # patch is cheap to re-cut. Without it a kernel-test record went stale on
                    # 2026-09-23 (milestone 315 moved `set_port_range_grant`) and only the weekly
                    # sweep saw it, as a line in a summary under a job that reported success.
                    applies = apply_checks[path]
                    if applies.returncode != 0:
                        problems.append(
                            "%s: `%s` no longer applies to this tree, so the covered code moved and "
                            "`%s` is `replayable` in name only. Re-cut the patch against the new code "
                            "and replay it (`cargo xtask test --arch <its Architecture:> --test %s` for a "
                            "kernel test, `cargo kani --harness` for a proof), or record the harness "
                            "`attested`/`unfalsified` until someone does. git said: %s"
                            % (where, path, h.name, h.name,
                               applies.stderr.strip().splitlines()[0] if applies.stderr.strip() else "?"))
        elif state == "attested":
            rest = h.record.split(None, 1)
            date = rest[1].split(".")[0].strip() if len(rest) > 1 else ""
            if not re.match(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$", date):
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` is `attested` with no `YYYY-MM-DD` date"
                                % (where, h.name))
    gaps = []
    for p in patch_files(pkgs):
        if p in claimed:
            continue
        kind, detail = unclaimed(p, pkgs)
        if kind == "gap":
            gaps.append((p, detail))
        else:
            fn = os.path.basename(p)[: -len(".patch")]
            problems.append("%s: %s. §134 checks both directions: a patch is named "
                            "`<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch` and the harness `%s` must "
                            "carry `Falsification: replayable %s`." % (p, detail, fn, p))
    return problems, gaps


def table(hs, pkgs, kts=()):
    counts = {s: 0 for s in STATES}
    by_pkg = {}
    for h in hs:
        counts[h.state] = counts.get(h.state, 0) + 1
        by_pkg.setdefault(h.package, []).append(h)
    width = max(len(h.name) for h in hs) if hs else 10
    for pkg in sorted(by_pkg):
        print("%s" % pkg)
        for h in by_pkg[pkg]:
            rec = h.record or "(no block)"
            print("  %-*s  %s" % (width, h.name, rec))
        print("")
    total = len(hs)
    evidence = counts.get("replayable", 0)
    print("%d harnesses in %d packages" % (total, len(by_pkg)))
    for s in STATES:
        n = counts.get(s, 0)
        print("  %-12s %3d  %4.0f%%" % (s, n, 100.0 * n / total if total else 0))
    print("")
    # The one number §134 asked this script to print, in the sentence it is honest in. Everything
    # else here is a means to it.
    print("%d of %d harnesses (%.0f%%) carry evidence a machine can replay." % (evidence, total, 100.0 * evidence / total if total else 0))
    # Records that exist, that a person performed, and that no sweep can replay, printed rather
    # than folded into either number above. Milestone 212 decided this explicitly: a denominator
    # that quietly excludes them is what it was written to end, and so is a numerator that quietly
    # includes evidence nothing re-runs.
    # The kernel-test half, printed as its own paragraph rather than folded into the ratio above
    # (milestone 305). Two reasons and neither is tidiness. `--count` feeds `script/lint`'s drift
    # check against `script/metrics`, which counts Kani harnesses from source text, so a merged
    # number would make two honest derivations disagree. And the evidence is not the same grade: a
    # Kani record is re-checked in a second and a kernel record costs a boot, so they are re-checked
    # at different cadences and a reader should be told which they are looking at.
    if kts:
        print("")
        print("%d kernel test(s) carry a falsification record, swept by booting one architecture:"
              % len(kts))
        kwidth = max(len(t.name) for t in kts)
        for t in kts:
            print("  %-*s  %s" % (kwidth, t.name, t.record))
        n = sum(1 for t in kts if t.state == "replayable")
        print("  %d of %d replayable. These are NOT in the ratio above." % (n, len(kts)))
    _, gaps = check(hs, pkgs, kts)
    if gaps:
        print("")
        print("%d falsification record(s) nothing here can sweep, because the thing they falsify is"
              % len(gaps))
        print("not a Kani harness. They are outside both numbers above:")
        for p, detail in gaps:
            print("  %s" % p)
            print("      %s" % detail)

def unfalsified(hs):
    """The worklist. Biggest package first, because §134's own examples of chaff are the crates with
    the most harnesses, and because falsifying two harnesses in one crate is one context load."""
    rows = [h for h in hs if h.state in (None, "unfalsified")]
    by_pkg = {}
    for h in rows:
        by_pkg.setdefault(h.package, []).append(h)
    for pkg in sorted(by_pkg, key=lambda c: (-len(by_pkg[c]), c)):
        print("%s  (%d)" % (pkg, len(by_pkg[pkg])))
        for h in by_pkg[pkg]:
            print("  %s  %s:%d" % (h.name, h.rel, h.line))
    print("")
    print("%d of %d harnesses have no falsification." % (len(rows), len(hs)))


def dirty():
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "status", "--porcelain"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
    return out


# **A per-record wall-clock limit, because a replay that cannot reach a verdict held a runner for
# two hours.** Before this, one record's `cargo kani` ran as long as it liked, so a harness CBMC
# could not finish (#1156's `nifefs` weakened-guard record, an unbounded loop over a symbolic count)
# took the per-pull-request job to its 45-minute axe and the weekly sweep to its 120-minute one
# (falsifications.yml run 36031723556), and every record after it went unswept. A limit turns that
# into one `ERROR` line and the next record.
#
# **The number is chosen from the only full-tree timing there is**, that same run: 73 Kani records
# before the hang, median 1.6 s, 90th percentile 8.2 s, and a long tail in `machine_discovery`
# (116 s, 136 s, and 497 s for `every_hex_field_the_loader_writes_is_one_the_kernel_reads_back`,
# build included, on a hosted runner). One record was measured slower, locally: `package_archive`'s
# `a_short_file_is_refused` at 20 to 31 minutes, the same unbounded-loop shape as the `nifefs` one,
# and it got the same bound rather than a limit raised to fit it (#1243, red in 1.3 s since). 15
# minutes is about 1.8 times the slowest record measured after that, so a legitimate record near the limit is a finding about that record, and it is a
# third of the per-pull-request job's 45 minutes, so one hung record still leaves the job time to
# finish the rest. A kernel test gets 30 minutes, which is `ci.yml`'s own bound on building the
# kernel and running the whole suite: a replay rebuilds whatever its patch touches and then runs
# ONE test, so it cannot legitimately need more than the job that runs all of them.
#
# `FALSIFICATIONS_RECORD_LIMIT` (seconds) overrides both, for a person who wants a hang reported
# faster or a slow record given room. It is read once and a bad value is refused, not defaulted.
#
# **A timeout is `error`, never `red` and never `survivor`.** No verdict is not evidence either
# way: red would claim the harness caught a defect it never finished checking, and survivor would
# claim it stayed green when it never finished at all.
LIMIT_KANI = 15 * 60
LIMIT_KERNEL_TEST = 30 * 60
_override = os.environ.get("FALSIFICATIONS_RECORD_LIMIT")
if _override is not None:
    if not _override.isdigit() or int(_override) == 0:
        sys.exit("script/falsifications: FALSIFICATIONS_RECORD_LIMIT must be a positive number of "
                 "seconds, not %r" % _override)
    LIMIT_KANI = LIMIT_KERNEL_TEST = int(_override)


def run_bounded(argv, limit):
    """Run `argv` for at most `limit` seconds. Returns (returncode, output, elapsed, timed_out).

    **The whole process group is killed, not the child.** `subprocess.run(timeout=...)` kills only
    the process it started, and that is `cargo`: `cbmc` (or QEMU, for a kernel test) is a grandchild
    and would carry on holding a core and several gigabytes after the sweep had moved on. So the
    replay starts in its own session and the group is signalled, TERM first so QEMU exits the way
    AGENTS.md asks, then KILL for whatever ignored it.
    """
    start = time.monotonic()
    p = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=root, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                         text=True, start_new_session=True)
    try:
        out, _ = p.communicate(timeout=limit)
        return p.returncode, out, time.monotonic() - start, False
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGKILL):
            try:
                os.killpg(p.pid, sig)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                break
            try:
                p.wait(timeout=10)
                break
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                continue
        out, _ = p.communicate()
        return None, out or "", time.monotonic() - start, True


def timed_out(limit, what):
    return "error", ("no verdict within the %d s limit; %s was killed. This is not evidence either "
                     "way: the record must be made to fail faster (see the header of "
                     "script/falsifications on the limit)" % (limit, what))


def replay_kani(h):
    """Run one Kani harness against the applied patch. Returns (verdict, detail).

    `--exact` against the fully qualified name, which is what the amended patch path buys beyond
    uniqueness: the path and this filter are one fact with the separators changed. Before the
    amendment this was a substring match on the bare function name, and `paging`'s three
    `index_is_always_in_bounds` harnesses would all have run, so "one of them went red" proved
    nothing about the one being falsified.
    """
    rc, out, _, hung = run_bounded(h.kani_argv(), LIMIT_KANI)
    if hung:
        return timed_out(LIMIT_KANI, "`cargo kani` and its CBMC")
    hit = re.search(r"(\d+) successfully verified harnesses, (\d+) failures, (\d+) total", out)
    if not hit:
        last = out.strip().splitlines()
        return "error", ("could not read Kani's summary; ran with exit %d%s"
                         % (rc, ("\n    " + last[-1]) if last else ""))
    if hit.group(3) != "1":
        # Belt and braces over `--exact`: if Kani ever matched none (a renamed harness) or more
        # than one, "something went red" would prove nothing about this harness.
        return "error", "`--exact %s` matched %s harnesses, not 1" % (h.qualified, hit.group(3))
    if hit.group(2) == "1":
        return "red", "the harness caught it"
    return "survivor", ""


def replay_kernel_test(t):
    """Boot one architecture, run one kernel test against the applied patch (milestone 305).

    **A non-zero exit is not enough to call this red**, and that is the whole of the care here. A
    patch that does not compile, a kernel that dies before the suite, a filter that selects nothing:
    every one of those exits non-zero and none of them is evidence about a confinement claim. So the
    transcript must first show that the kernel booted and selected exactly one test, which is
    `running 1 of N tests (filter: ...)`, printed by `testing::runner` after the filter is applied.
    Only then does the exit code mean what it looks like it means.

    A test that SKIPS is neither red nor a survivor in any useful sense and is reported as an error,
    because a skip means the fixture the claim needs was not attached to this boot (no RedoxFS disk,
    no device page) and the falsification was never actually attempted. Reading that as green would
    be the manufactured fact milestone 214's skip accounting exists to refuse, one level out.

    What it cannot check is the one thing milestone 202 warns about: that the red came through the
    assertion the patch's prose predicts. The panic line is returned so a reader can judge, which is
    rung three rather than rung two, and this script's BUGS says so.
    """
    rc, out, _, hung = run_bounded(t.replay_argv(), LIMIT_KERNEL_TEST)
    if hung:
        return timed_out(LIMIT_KERNEL_TEST, "the build and boot")
    selected = re.search(r"running (\d+) of (\d+) tests", out)
    if selected is None:
        last = [l for l in out.strip().splitlines() if l.strip()]
        return "error", ("the kernel never reached its test runner on %s (exit %d): the patch may "
                         "not compile, or the boot died.%s"
                         % (t.architecture, rc,
                            ("\n    " + last[-1]) if last else ""))
    if selected.group(1) != "1":
        return "error", ("`--test %s` selected %s tests, not 1: a red would not be about this one"
                         % (t.name, selected.group(1)))
    result = re.search(r"^test result:.*$", out, re.M)
    if result is not None and "skipped" in result.group(0):
        return "error", ("the test SKIPPED on %s (%s): its fixture was not attached to this boot, "
                         "so the falsification was never attempted"
                         % (t.architecture, result.group(0).strip()))
    if rc == 0:
        return "survivor", "it ran, it passed, and the defect is still in the tree under the patch"
    # The panic's location AND its message, which is what a reader needs to tell a claim-shaped red
    # from milestone 202's watchdog timeout at 234 seconds. `panicked at <file>:<line>:` is on its
    # own line and the assertion's own sentence is on the next ones, so taking only the first gives
    # a coordinate where the point is the words.
    hit = re.search(r"^.*panicked at.*$", out, re.M)
    if hit is None:
        return "red", ("exit %d on %s, with no panic line in the transcript: read it before "
                       "believing this" % (rc, t.architecture))
    # The assertion's own sentence follows the location on the next lines, and so, eventually, does
    # cargo's own chatter, because this runs a build and a boot down one pipe. Stop at the first
    # line that is plainly the toolchain talking rather than the kernel.
    lines = out[hit.start():].splitlines()[:5]
    body = []
    for line in lines:
        s = line.strip()
        if not s or re.match(r"^(warning|error|note|help|\||=|-->|\^)", s):
            break
        body.append(s)
    return "red", "the test caught it on %s\n      %s" % (t.architecture, "\n      ".join(body))


def sweep(hs, only=None):
    """Apply each recorded patch, run that ONE harness, require red, revert.

    A survivor here is a harness whose own recorded falsification failed to make it fail, which is
    exactly the signal nothing in this tree could previously detect.
    """
    d = dirty()
    if d:
        print("script/falsifications --sweep refuses to run on a dirty working tree: it applies and")
        print("reverts patches with `git apply`, and a failure mid-run would leave a deliberate")
        print("defect in your source. Commit or set the change aside first.")
        print("")
        print(d)
        return 2
    todo = [h for h in hs if h.state == "replayable" and (only is None or h.package in only)]
    if not todo:
        print("nothing to sweep" + (" in %s" % ", ".join(sorted(only)) if only else ""))
        return 0
    survivors, broken, hung = [], [], []
    for h in todo:
        patch = os.path.join(root, h.patch)
        print("==> %s::%s" % (h.package, h.name))
        applied = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "apply", patch],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True)
        if applied.returncode != 0:
            # A patch that will not apply is a FAILURE and not a skip. §134's claim is that this rot
            # is the mechanism working: the covered code moved, so the falsification is stale and
            # must be redone rather than trusted.
            print("    STALE    the patch no longer applies; the covered code moved")
            print("    " + applied.stderr.strip().replace("\n", "\n    "))
            broken.append(h)
            continue
        try:
            replay = replay_kernel_test if h.kind == "kernel test" else replay_kani
            began = time.monotonic()
            verdict, detail = replay(h)
            # Every verdict carries its wall-clock time, build included, because the limit above
            # was chosen from the one run whose timestamps happened to survive, and the next
            # person to move it should have a whole sweep to read instead.
            took = "(%.1f s)" % (time.monotonic() - began)
            if verdict == "red":
                print("    red      %s %s" % (detail, took))
            elif verdict == "survivor":
                print("    SURVIVOR it stayed green with its own falsification applied %s" % took)
                if detail:
                    print("    " + detail)
                survivors.append(h)
            else:
                print("    ERROR    %s %s" % (detail, took))
                (hung if detail.startswith("no verdict within") else broken).append(h)
        finally:
            rev = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "apply", "-R", patch],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True)
        if rev.returncode != 0:
            # Stop here rather than continue. Every later harness would be proved against a tree
            # holding a deliberate defect, and a green result from that means nothing.
            print("    COULD NOT REVERT %s; your tree is modified" % h.patch)
            print("    " + rev.stderr.strip())
            return 3
    print("")
    print("%d swept, %d survivors, %d stale or broken, %d without a verdict"
          % (len(todo), len(survivors), len(broken), len(hung)))
    for h in survivors:
        print("  survivor  %s::%s" % (h.package, h.name))
    for h in broken:
        print("  stale     %s::%s" % (h.package, h.name))
    for h in hung:
        print("  no verdict %s::%s (limit %d s)"
              % (h.package, h.name, LIMIT_KERNEL_TEST if h.kind == "kernel test" else LIMIT_KANI))
    return 1 if (survivors or broken or hung) else 0


def affected_packages(base, hs):
    """Which harness packages a diff can reach, by the rule `script/verify --affected-since` uses.

    Package-granular on purpose and stated in BUGS: nothing here knows which lines a harness covers,
    so a change inside a harness package's dependency closure re-falsifies every replayable harness
    in it. It fails toward doing too much, which for a run measured in seconds is the right
    direction.
    """
    changed = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "diff", "--name-only", base, "HEAD"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.split()
    meta = metadata(no_deps=False)
    ws = meta["workspace_root"]
    pkgs = {}
    for p in meta["packages"]:
        rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.dirname(p["manifest_path"]), ws)
        if rel == "." or rel.startswith(".."):
            continue
        pkgs[p["id"]] = (p["name"], rel + "/")

    def owner(path):
        best = None
        for pid, (_, prefix) in pkgs.items():
            if path.startswith(prefix) and (best is None or len(prefix) > len(pkgs[best][1])):
                best = pid
        return best

    changed_ids = set()
    unattributable = False
    for f in changed:
        if re.match(r"^(notes/|design/|[A-Z_]+\.md$|script/|helpers/|\.github/)", f):
            continue
        pid = owner(f)
        if pid is None:
            unattributable = True
        else:
            changed_ids.add(pid)
    edges = {n["id"]: [d["pkg"] for d in n.get("deps", [])] for n in meta["resolve"]["nodes"]}
    carriers = {h.package for h in hs}
    out = set()
    for pid, (name, _) in pkgs.items():
        if name not in carriers:
            continue
        if unattributable:
            out.add(name)
            continue
        closure, stack = set(), [pid]
        while stack:
            cur = stack.pop()
            if cur in closure:
                continue
            closure.add(cur)
            stack.extend(edges.get(cur, []))
        if closure & changed_ids:
            out.add(name)
    return out


pkgs = packages()
hs = harnesses(pkgs)
kts = kernel_tests(pkgs)
if not hs:
    sys.exit("script/falsifications: found no Kani harnesses in any workspace package, which cannot "
             "be right")

if mode == "--check":
    problems, gaps = check(hs, pkgs, kts)
    for p in problems:
        print("  " + p)
    if problems:
        print("")
        print("%d problem(s). See the header of script/falsifications and DECISIONS §134." % len(problems))
        sys.exit(1)
    n = sum(1 for h in hs if h.state == "replayable")
    print("  %d harnesses in %d packages, every one carrying a Falsification: block (%d replayable)"
          % (len(hs), len({h.package for h in hs}), n))
    if kts:
        print("  %d kernel test(s) carry a record (%d replayable), swept by booting one architecture"
              % (len(kts), sum(1 for t in kts if t.state == "replayable")))
    for p, _ in gaps:
        print("  %s falsifies something that is not a Kani harness; nothing sweeps it" % p)
elif mode == "--count":
    # Two integers on one line, harnesses and how many carry evidence, for `script/lint`'s drift
    # check (milestone 236: three derivations are copied between scripts, and nothing notices when
    # they drift). It exists so that check can compare THIS derivation rather than a restatement of
    # it: `script/metrics` counts the same harnesses from source text alone, because it reads blobs
    # at revisions nobody has checked out and cannot run the `cargo metadata` walk above. A check
    # that reimplemented either side would be a third copy of the thing it was policing.
    #
    # Machine-readable rather than the prose `--check` already prints, because a gate that greps a
    # sentence breaks the day somebody improves the sentence.
    print("%d %d" % (len(hs), sum(1 for h in hs if h.state in ("replayable", "attested"))))
elif mode == "--unfalsified":
    unfalsified(hs)
elif mode == "--sweep":
    # Kernel records join the sweep rather than getting a verb of their own, because a second verb
    # would make the honest ratio the one nobody runs. The cost is stated in BUGS: about a minute
    # each, against a harness's second.
    sys.exit(sweep(hs + kts, set(args[1:]) or None))
elif mode == "--affected-since":
    if len(args) < 2:
        sys.exit("usage: script/falsifications --affected-since <base-ref>")
    names = affected_packages(args[1], hs)
    if not names:
        print("no harness package is reachable from this change; nothing to re-falsify")
        sys.exit(0)
    print("reachable harness packages: %s" % ", ".join(sorted(names)))
    sys.exit(sweep(hs, names))
elif mode in ("", "--all"):
    table(hs, pkgs, kts)
else:
    sys.exit("script/falsifications: unknown option %r (see the header)" % mode)
